[69.12, 69.18, " Oh,"], [69.28, 69.42, " yep."], [69.6, 69.62, " I"], [69.62, 69.7, " don't"], [69.7, 69.82, " know"], [69.82, 69.94, " what"], [69.94, 70.12, " flavor"], [70.12, 70.36, " it"], [70.36, 70.52, " is,"], [70.52, 71.14, " but..."], [71.14, 71.3, " Cool"], [71.3, 71.44, " blue."], [71.76, 71.86, " The"], [71.86, 71.98, " blue"], [71.98, 72.2, " color."], [72.4, 72.46, " Yeah."], [72.98, 72.98, " Yeah."], [73.16, 73.16, " Same"], [73.16, 73.26, " you."], [73.34, 73.5, " The"], [73.5, 73.62, " light"], [73.62, 73.78, " blue"], [73.78, 73.92, " one."], [74.06, 74.2, " Glace"], [74.2, 74.32, " your"], [74.32, 74.5, " freeze,"], [74.7, 74.78, " I"], [74.78, 74.94, " believe."], [75.14, 75.4, " Glace"], [75.4, 75.54, " your"], [75.54, 75.7, " freeze."], [75.9, 75.92, " There"], [75.92, 76.0, " we"], [76.0, 76.18, " go."], [76.26, 76.26, " Glace"], [76.26, 76.26, " your"], [76.26, 76.48, " freeze."], [76.76, 76.82, " Thank"], [76.82, 76.92, " you"], [76.92, 77.0, " for"], [77.0, 77.16, " joining"], [77.16, 77.32, " us."], [77.46, 77.54, " Thanks"], [77.54, 77.72, " for"], [77.72, 77.88, " having"], [77.88, 78.06, " me."]]}, "caps": {"7": "Ep. 7 Brett Squires \u26a1\ufe0f\u26be\ufe0f \n\nBrett breaks down how he fuels his performance on game days. He talks about his caffeine use which helps him get dialed in for games. Caffeine is proven to increase focus, alertness, and reaction time, which are all important qualities in the game of baseball. Brett\u2019s pre-game caffeine routine helps him prepare and perform on the diamond, but this sort of caffeine routine and/or reliance shouldn\u2019t be done by the average person.\n\nThank you for your time and input! @brettsquires_ \n\nFollow so you don\u2019t miss more fitness/nutrition info!", "6": "Ep. 6 Sam Kulasingam \u2708\ufe0f\u26be\ufe0f \n\nSam explains his mid-inning routine when it comes to fueling his performance on the diamond.  As a utility man on defense and someone who is consistently on the bases offensively, he burns lots of calories over the course of the game. He stays fueled by leaning on Gatorade and water for hydration and electrolytes, as well as fruit snacks and apple sauce which are great for that quick energy burst from the simple sugars they contain. Sam also shares that his favorite Gatorade is the blue one, specifically glacier freeze, as \u201cit does not miss.\u201d Just like his bat hitting the ball. \n\nThank you for your time and input! @samkula_ \n\nFollow so you don\u2019t miss episode 7\ud83d\udc4d\ud83c\udffc", "5": "Ep. 5 Carter Jensen \ud83d\udc51 \u26be\ufe0f \n\nCarter shares with us a post-game cheat meal he enjoys after catching behind the plate and hitting HRs to help the Royals win on a nightly basis. Catching is one of the more strenuous positions on the diamond, so it\u2019s important to find ways to get the same amount of calories in (or more) that you\u2019re losing from performance to avoid weight loss. This is especially important over the course of a 162 game regular season. Carter enjoys chocolate milk which is an excellent way to replenish calories burned either post-workout, or in this case, post-game. He also enjoys pizza which is high in calories, making it an effective way for him to replenish the energy he burned during the game.\n\nThank you for your time and input! @carterjensenn \n\nFollow so you don\u2019t miss episode 5\ud83d\udc4d\ud83c\udffc", "4": "Ep. 4 Olivia Lightholder \ud83d\udce3 \n\nLivvy keeps it short and sweet, she shares her favorite halftime snacks are zebra cakes and oatmeal cream pies.  She chooses to fuel her performance by enjoying some simple carbs, which are good for immediate energy. The consumption of these snacks should be limited however because they are made from processed flours and refined sugars, which cause a blood sugar spike, leading to an eventual crash. \n\nThank you for your time and input! @livvylightholder \n@vol_cheer \n\nFollow so you don\u2019t miss episode 5 \ud83d\udc4d\ud83c\udffc", "3": "Ep. 3 Jared Dickey \ud83d\udc18 \u26be\ufe0f \n\nJared discusses how isn\u2019t allowed to have snacks in the dugout during the game. His alternative is to take advantage of the bubble gum and seeds that are in the dugout during the game. Bubble gum is an option to get quick sugars in the blood for more of an immediate energy source. Sunflower seeds are typically high in sodium which aid in hydration as Na (sodium) binds to H2O and helps your cells stay hydrated. Both options are a feasible option to no snacks in the dugout. \n\nThank you for your time and input! @jared.dickey \n\nFollow so you don\u2019t miss episode 4 \ud83d\udc4d\ud83c\udffc", "2": "Ep. 2 Carson \u201cRic\u201d Roccaforte\n\nCarson is a real pro. He tries \u201cnot to cheat too much\u201d but when he does he enjoys some Raising Canes. Carson prioritizes healthy eating habits off the diamond so he can continually help his team win on the diamond. According to teammate @jjohnson_12 Mr. Roccaforte \u201ceats lots of protein so he can hit lots of HRs.\u201d Invest now while you can @raisingcanes \n\nThank you for your time and input! @carson_roccaforte7 \n\nFollow so you don\u2019t miss episode 3 \ud83d\udc4d\ud83c\udffc", "1": "Ep. 1 Justin \u201cJJ\u201d Johnson\n\nJustin shares a little insight on how he prioritizes carbohydrates before competition to give him the energy needed to make diving plays all over the diamond and hit pull-side HRs to help his team win on a nightly basis. His teammate @carson_roccaforte7 suggests that Justin routinely eats chipotle to fuel his performance. \n\nThank you for your time and input! @jjohnson_12 \n\nFollow so you don\u2019t miss episode 2 \ud83d\udc4d\ud83c\udffc"}}